WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.560
<v Ira Glass>We'll call this guy Chris. He had a job as a middleman, but a kind of odd sort of middleman.
<v Ira Glass>我们叫这个家伙克里斯。他以前从事中介工作，但是一种很奇特的中介。

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.850
<v Chris>We basically are the middleman between the deaf and someone who can hear.
<v Chris>我们基本上是聋人和听力正常人之间的中间人。

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:32.970
<v Ira Glass>Chris worked for a company that helped deaf people communicate over the telephone. To make a call, a deaf person would contact Chris. He would get the hearing person on the line. The deaf person would type what it is that they wanted to say. Chris would read this to the hearing person, and then type their responses back to the deaf person. Most of the conversations were what you'd expect: people calling their families, talking about what they had for dinner, calling their banks.
<v Ira Glass>克里斯在一家帮助聋人通过电话进行交流的公司工作。如果聋人想打电话，他们会联系克里斯。克里斯会拨通对方的电话，让听力正常的人接听。聋人会打字写下他们想说的话，克里斯会读给对方听，然后把对方的回答打字传回给聋人。大多数对话都在意料之中：人们给家人打电话，谈论晚餐吃了什么，给银行打电话。

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:55.650
<v Chris>And then again, because I usually worked nights, some of the calls were very intimate phone conversations between people breaking up, people just falling in love for the first time, people telling them that members of their family had died, phone sex, things no one would ever want you to hear, really.
<v Chris>话虽如此，因为我通常上夜班，有些电话是非常私密的通话，比如情侣分手、第一次坠入爱河、告诉家人亲属过世的消息、电话性爱等等，真的，这些都是别人绝不希望你听到的事情。

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:05.800
<v Ira Glass>One thing that happened more often than you'd think, especially when parents were talking to their kids, is that they would get into an argument, and then one of them would try to drag Chris into the argument on their side of it.
<v Ira Glass>有一件事发生的频率比你想象的要高得多，尤其是在父母与孩子交谈时，他们会发生争执，然后其中一方会试图把克里斯拉进争执中，站到他们那边。

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:19.580
<v Chris>And I wasn't allowed to say anything, so I would just say, the CA, we're co-communication assistants, does not have that information. That was the blanket phrase that they let me use.
<v Chris>我被规定不能发表意见，所以我通常会说，CA，也就是沟通辅助员，没有这方面的信息。这就是他们让我使用的万能回应。

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.200
<v Ira Glass>That's so computer-like.
<v Ira Glass>那听起来太像计算机了。

00:01:21.200 --> 00:02:00.290
<v Chris>Well, that was the paradigm they wanted us to follow. They wanted us to be computers, basically, information machines. I remember a daughter had been, it was a deaf daughter, and she had been out sleeping around and doing a lot of drugs, evidently. And the mother was just trying to tell her that she needed to stop doing all these things and saying, "You may feel like you're young, and you can just do what you want, and since you're deaf, you feel you have an excuse to do all these things."
<v Chris>嗯，那就是他们希望我们遵循的模式。他们基本上希望我们成为计算机，成为信息机器。我记得有个聋人女儿，显然她一直在外面乱搞，还吸食大量毒品。母亲只是试图告诉她需要停止这些行为，说：“你可能觉得你还年轻，可以随心所欲，而且因为你是聋人，你就觉得你有借口做这些事。”

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:19.000
<v Chris>And she started talking to me, and she said, "You don't do drugs, do you?" And I said, the CA does not have that information. And then she said, "Well, can't you just type to her and tell her that you think she's hurting herself as well?"
<v Chris>她开始跟我说话，她说：“你没吸毒，对吧？”我说，CA没有这方面的信息。然后她说：“好吧，那你不能直接打字告诉她，你也认为她在伤害自己吗？”

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.360
<v Ira Glass>Now when she's saying this to you, do you have to type that in, so the daughter knows that she's saying it to you?
<v Ira Glass>现在，当她对你说这些话时，你必须把这些打出来，让那个女儿知道她在对你说这些话吗？

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:55.520
<v Chris>Yeah, you do. You have to type everything that she's saying. The daughter really disliked that. The daughter would start talking to me and say, "Don't let her talk to you." And she would start trying to tell me just how terrible the mother had been to her growing up. The mother actually said at this point, "This is really embarrassing," and tried to pretend like I wasn't there the rest of the time.
<v Chris>是的，必须。你必须打出她说的每一句话。那个女儿真的很讨厌这样。女儿开始跟我说：“别让她跟你说话。”她还试图告诉我，她母亲在她的成长过程中有多么糟糕。母亲这时说：“这真的很尴尬，”并试图假装我在其余时间都不存在。

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:15.170
<v Ira Glass>It's hard to be the middleman. Pizza delivery places always hung up on Chris and the other operators. Phone sex operators and drug dealers were way more service-oriented when it came to the deaf. And then there were the people who liked to mess with the middleman, like two African-American guys one day, who started using all sorts of words and phrases just because it would be funny to hear the white operator say them.
<v Ira Glass>做中间人很难。披萨店总是挂断克里斯和其他接线员的电话。在对待聋人方面，电话性爱接线员和毒贩的服务意识要强得多。还有一些人喜欢捉弄中间人，比如有一天两个非裔美国人，他们开始使用各种词汇和短语，只是觉得听白人接线员说这些话会很有趣。

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:32.820
<v Chris>They were talking in really heavy street slang. One person was intent on having me use the n-word, over and over. And it was very uncomfortable. But the two people using it on the phone obviously didn't care. They just wanted to have me do it.
<v Chris>他们用非常浓重的街头俚语交谈。其中一个人一心想让我反复使用那个歧视黑人的词。这非常令人不安。但在电话里使用这个词的两个人显然不在乎。他们只是想让我说出口。

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:47.310
<v Chris>And they were even joking about me doing it. They would just be laughing. A lot of people find a lot of amusement out of playing with a Communication Assistant. I found it kind of funny after a while.
<v Chris>他们甚至拿我这么做来开玩笑。他们只是在那儿大笑。很多人从戏弄沟通辅助员中找到了乐趣。过了一段时间，我也觉得这有点滑稽。

00:03:47.310 --> 00:04:06.790
<v Chris>All of my friends who went through the training for this were young black women. And they thought I was kind of really straight. And they used to perk their ears up immediately as soon as they heard me get into any sort of ghetto speak, or whatnot.
<v Chris>所有和我一起接受这项培训的朋友都是年轻的黑人女性。她们觉得我非常“正经”。只要她们听到我用任何一种贫民窟的口吻说话，她们就会立刻竖起耳朵。

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:29.250
<v Chris>Because I can't do it. I didn't like it. But I wasn't going to read in a completely white bread tone of voice, either. I didn't want to do that. It was humiliating and funny at the same time.
<v Chris>因为我做不到。我不喜欢那样。但我也不想用一种完全“纯白”的语调去读。我不想那样做。这既令人羞辱又令人觉得滑稽。

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:34.920
<v Ira Glass>Give another example of times it was hard just to be an information machine.
<v Ira Glass>再举个例子，说说作为一台信息机器有多难。

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:55.180
<v Chris>A man actually had just fallen off a ladder in his garage. And he had gone deaf. And so he was calling up all his friends and telling them what had happened to him. And so these people were completely unfamiliar with the service.
<v Chris>有个男人刚从车库的梯子上摔下来。他失聪了。所以他给所有朋友打电话，告诉他们发生了什么事。而这些人对这项服务完全陌生。

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:05.640
<v Chris>So I had to explain the service. They still didn't understand it. And then he had to tell them, explain what the service was, why he was using it, and how he had just gone deaf.
<v Chris>所以我不得不解释这项服务。他们还是不理解。然后他不得不告诉他们，解释这项服务是什么，为什么要使用它，以及他刚是如何失聪的。

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:32.720
<v Chris>And it was very obvious, I remember one man he had called, they were not that close. And the man obviously did not know how to react. He was having a really hard time. He just kept saying I'm sorry, over and over. And it was really excessive. And it was just really hard to keep telling all of it, knowing that you're going to have to tell six or seven people after this that this man has gone deaf.
<v Chris>显而易见，我记得他打给的一个人，他们关系并不亲近。那个人显然不知道该如何反应。他表现得非常痛苦。他只是不停地说抱歉，一遍又一遍。这真的太夸张了。在知道接下来还要告诉六七个人这个男人失聪的消息时，还要一直复述这些，真的太难了。

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:50.510
<v Ira Glass>And at the end of that, so now you as a person, you've been through this experience with him of now, the two of you have told a half dozen people that he's gone deaf. It must be hard that after you get off the phone with the last person, that you and he don't talk about what just happened.
<v Ira Glass>在这之后，作为一个人，你和他一起经历了这件事，你们两个人告诉了六个人他失聪的消息。在与最后一个人通话结束后，你们两人却不能谈论刚才发生的事，这一定很难受吧。

00:05:50.510 --> 00:06:18.870
<v Chris>Right. Sometimes people will say, because there is a 30-second window of time where I'm still on the line with him. I remember he typed, "Thank you so much. I'm sure that must have been very hard for you as well as me." So there is a brief moment of time.
<v Chris>没错。有时候人们会说，因为我有30秒的时间还在线上陪着他。我记得他打字说：“非常感谢你。我相信这对你来说一定和我一样难受。”所以确实有那么短暂的一刻。

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:29.820
<v Ira Glass>It's hard to be the middleman. It's hard to keep your feelings out of it. It hard to stay neutral. People want you to get involved, and they want you to get out of it. You can't win for losing. And I say this as a middle child.
<v Ira Glass>做中间人很难。很难让自己置身事外。很难保持中立。人们希望你参与其中，又希望你别插手。怎么做都不对。我是家里的老二，所以我深有体会。

00:06:29.820 --> 00:07:00.690
<v Ira Glass>And there there's that whole "eliminate the middleman" thing, eliminate the middleman. If you Google the phrase "eliminate the middleman," you get 3,800 websites promising to eliminate middlemen of every kind for you, car dealers, realtors, stockbrokers, furniture stores, stamp sales, plus one site that has figured out a way to bypass the lousy crop of presidential candidates we're offered every four years. Eliminate the middleman, it promises. Satan for President in 2004.
<v Ira Glass>还有那种所谓的“消灭中间人”的说法。如果你在谷歌上搜索“消灭中间人”这个短语，你会找到3800个网站，承诺为你消灭各种中间人：汽车经销商、房地产经纪人、股票经纪人、家具店、邮票销售商，还有一个网站想出了绕过我们每四年都要面对的那一堆糟糕总统候选人的办法。消灭中间人，它承诺道。2004年选撒旦当总统。

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:31.710
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. Today on our program, middlemen. We will not eliminate him. We will not pass the savings on to you. No, no, no, for once, a defense of the beleaguered middleman in three acts, including a simple explanation of how you, yes, you, could have gotten rich off Michael Jordan, using the simple rules of market economics and a good down parka. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好了，这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际发行。今天节目的主题是：中间人。我们不会消灭他。我们不会把节省下来的钱转让给你。不，不，不，这一次，我们要为处境艰难的中间人辩护，节目分三幕进行，包括一个简单的解释，告诉你——没错，就是你——如何利用市场经济的简单规则和一件好的防寒派克大衣，从迈克尔·乔丹身上发财。留下来吧。

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:54.550
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Show Me the Monet. You know, you can't do a story about middlemen without a story about business and all the people who make their livings buying low and selling high, how they are sort of looking out for their customers, and sort of looking out for their product and their suppliers, and mainly looking out for themselves. And this story begins, as so many radio stories seem to these days, with a classifieds ad.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，给我看莫奈。你知道，如果不讲关于生意、不讲那些靠低买高卖为生的人，就做不成关于中间人的故事。他们如何在某种程度上照顾顾客，在某种程度上照顾产品和供应商，主要是照顾他们自己。这个故事，就像最近很多广播故事一样，从一则分类广告开始。

00:07:54.550 --> 00:08:11.520
<v Man>Triple-A tickets, all events, seven days, concerts, sports, theater, Lincoln Park. And then it's got the address and the phone number. For a lot of years, I worked here. And if someone had called this ad, called in the phone number here, I probably would have been the one to answer the phone.
<v Man>AAA门票，所有活动，七天，音乐会，体育，戏剧，林肯公园。然后是地址和电话号码。我在这里工作了很多年。如果有人打了这则广告上的电话，打到了这里，接电话的人很可能就是我。

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:46.000
<v Man>When people call this number, I don't know what kind of place they're imagining, maybe a fancy little office downtown. But we were actually in the back of a video store, in this little dark office, the ceiling fan going real slowly counter-clockwise, kind of squeaking, one beam of light coming through the tall window up top, and dust streaming around the room, and a dog's barking out back. I remember, for a while, there was not many movies on the shelves. There was Buster, that movie that Phil Collins made.
<v Man>当人们拨打这个号码时，我不知道他们想象的是什么样的地方，也许是市中心一间精致的小办公室。但实际上我们是在一家录像带店的后面，在一个昏暗的小办公室里，吊扇很慢地逆时针转动，发出吱吱声，一束光从顶上的高窗透进来，灰尘在房间里飘荡，后院还有狗在叫。我记得有一段时间，货架上的电影不多。有一部叫《巴斯特》的电影，是菲尔·柯林斯拍的。

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.270
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[笑声]

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:57.870
<v Man>And no one would ever rent those movies.
<v Man>而且根本没人会租那些电影。

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.400
<v Alex Blumberg>How long did you work there?
<v Alex Blumberg>你在那儿干了多久？

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:21.020
<v Man>On and off from '96 to about 2001. Over the course of those few years, I got to know the ticket world inside and out. I met people with names like 35th Street Eddie, South Side Frank, Fast Willie, Richie the Head. There was a guy named Elbow, who had sort of a big elbow.
<v Man>从96年到2001年左右，断断续续干了几年。在那几年里，我对票务圈了如指掌。我遇到了像35街埃迪、南区弗兰克、快手威利、头儿里奇这样的人。还有个叫“肘子”的人，因为他的肘关节特别大。

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:37.370
<v Man>A lot of guys got nicknames after whatever event that they specialized in, Monet Joe. One time, there was a Monet exhibit at the art institute. So people come in from out of town, and they want to go to the exhibit. The only way they can go is if they have these VIP staff passes.
<v Man>很多人是以他们专攻的活动来起绰号的，比如莫奈·乔。有一次，艺术学院有一个莫奈展览。人们从外地赶来，想去看展览。他们能进去的唯一办法就是拥有这些贵宾员工通行证。

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:57.260
<v Man>And Monet somehow managed to have a few hundred of them. All the ticket brokers, all the ticket guys, all the hotel concierges, everyone was calling Monet. And from then on, he's just been called Monet.
<v Man>莫奈不知怎么地弄到了几百张。所有的票务经纪人、所有的票务商、所有的酒店礼宾部，每个人都在给莫奈打电话。从那以后，他就一直被称为莫奈。

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:06.160
<v Alex Blumberg>So you came back to town and started to look up some of the people that you used to work with, right? And so who did you talk to first?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以你回到城里，开始联系一些你以前的同事，对吧？那么你先和谁谈了？

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.430
<v Man>First, I looked up an old ticket friend named Liz. And we started talking about some of our old clients.
<v Man>首先，我联系了一位老票务朋友莉兹。我们开始谈论我们的一些老客户。

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:27.960
<v Liz>You spends so much time on the phone talking to the worst people. They're either mean, or they're rude. Ugh. And you're always putting people on hold, like, "Let me get my supervisor. Let me talk to my supervisor." And you're like, ptth. I'm not doing [BLEEP] for this guy. He stayed on hold. He'll hang up. He'll hang up.
<v Liz>你花那么多时间在电话里和最糟糕的人交谈。他们要么很刻薄，要么很粗鲁。额。你总是让人们等待，比如，“让我去找我的主管。让我和我的主管谈谈。”而你会想，呸。我才不会为这家伙做任何事呢。让他等着。他会挂断的。他会挂断的。

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:48.690
<v Man>We call it the stew pot. When you left someone just on hold, you say let them stew for a  little while. And usually, if they're kind of hemming and hawing, going back and forth, you let them stew for a few, like five minutes. Maybe come on once and be like, "Hang in there, man. Hang in there. I'll be right back with you." Let them stew another five minutes. Then, after 10, they're ready to talk. They're ready to deal.
<v Man>我们称之为“炖锅”。当你把某人晾在一边等待时，你说让他们炖一会儿。通常如果他们犹豫不决，反复推脱，你就让他们炖一会儿，比如五分钟。也许偶尔上线说一下：“坚持住，伙计。坚持住。我马上回来。”让他们再炖五分钟。然后十分钟后，他们就准备好谈话了。他们准备好交易了。

00:10:48.690 --> 00:11:09.330
<v Liz>Yeah, definitely. We had the lead transfer him to this line that would ring and ring and ring. You're like, "Don't pick up line four. Everybody knows line four is [BLEEP] line. Don't pick it up."
<v Liz>是啊，绝对的。我们会让主管把他们转到那条会一直响一直响的线路上。你会想，“别接四号线。每个人都知道四号线是烂线。别接。”

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:38.760
<v Man>That's so funny, because we always had the same thing. We had different lines that meant different things. You had a couple lines you had to answer different things. Like one line, you had to answer, hello. Like any time we try to get act tickets through legitimate means, like group sales or anything like that. You would put the home phone number. So it said on all the phones, hello. There was a little taped-up piece of paper that said "hello" on it to remind you to answer it "hello?"
<v Man>真搞笑，因为我们也总是遇到同样的情况。我们有不同的线路，意味着不同的含义。你有几条线路必须以不同的方式接听。比如有一条线，你必须接听时说“喂”。比如任何时候我们试图通过正规渠道获得门票时，比如团体预订之类的。你会填写家庭电话号码。所以所有电话上都写着“喂”。上面贴着一张小纸条，写着“喂”来提醒你接听时要说“喂？”。

00:11:38.760 --> 00:12:03.310
<v Man>This was a phone line that was supposed to simulate an actual home, someone's residence. You were just supposed to pick it up and say, "Hello?" This was when we ordered group sale tickets from the Cubs. So we put in orders for tons of group sales. And if they knew we were a ticket broker, they wouldn't be having it. We wouldn't get the tickets. So we'd pick different names of organizations.
<v Man>这是一条模拟真实家庭住宅的电话线。你只需要接起来说“喂？”。这是我们从小熊队订团体票的时候。所以我们预订了大量的团体票。如果他们知道我们是票务经纪人，他们就不会卖给我们了。我们买不到票。所以我们会用不同组织的名字。

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.600
<v Alex Blumberg>They actually would check, though?
<v Alex Blumberg>他们真的会核实吗？

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:39.880
<v Man>No, they would just call you. And they would be like, what's the name of your organization? And sometimes you would say, "Oh, well, it's Grandpa Louie's 70th birthday. So we're bringing 40 people to that game." Or, "The Little League hockey team that I coach, we're having a special slumber party. And we need 120 tickets. We got tykes coming from all over, up and down the state. They're all-stars. You should see some of these kids. They're really fine hockey players."
<v Man>不，他们只会打电话给你。他们会问，你们组织的名字是什么？有时候你会说：“哦，那是路易爷爷的70岁生日。所以我们要带40个人去那场比赛。”或者，“我执教的小联盟曲棍球队，我们要举行一个特别的睡衣派对。我们需要120张票。我们从州里四面八方找来了小球员。他们都是全明星。你应该看看这些孩子，他们是真正出色的曲棍球运动员。”

00:12:39.880 --> 00:13:18.170
<v Man>There was another phone line that if it rang, you were just supposed to pick the phone line up and not say anything at all, just pick it up and put it to your ear and be silent. That was Guy Lobster, who owned the business. That was one of his special lines. And it was important that just didn't say anything. You weren't supposed to let them hear your voice, even. So just pick it up and be silent. Actually, I never figured out what that one was for.
<v Man>还有另一条电话线，如果它响了，你只需要接起电话，什么都不要说，接起来放在耳边保持安静就行。那是盖伊·龙虾的线，他是老板。那是他的一条特殊线路。重要的是绝对不要说话。你不应该让他们听到你的声音。所以只需要接起来保持沉默。说实话，我从来没弄明白那条线是干嘛的。

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:42.340
<v Man>There's a guy named Leo, who was one of my best friends of all the ticket guys. And a couple days ago, I was in Ann Arbor, at a Michigan football game, and Leo was out there, buying and selling. I saw a couple of the old guys I knew, so they'd come in from all over. And Leo was out there. So I put this mic on his collar, attached the battery pack to his butt, and just listened to him walk around.
<v Man>有个叫里奥的家伙，他是我所有票务朋友中最好的一个。几天前，我在安娜堡看一场密歇根大学的足球赛，里奥也在那儿，忙着买进卖出。我看到了几个我认识的老伙计，他们从四面八方赶来。里奥也在那儿。于是我把麦克风夹在他的领子上，把电池包别在他的屁股上，然后就听他在周围走动。

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:47.200
<v Leo>Anybody got one extra ticket? Anybody got one extra?
<v Leo>有人有多余的票吗？有人有多余的吗？

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:50.590
<v Alex Blumberg>What is this called, actually, what he's doing now? Is there a term for what he's doing?
<v Alex Blumberg>这叫什么，他现在在做什么？他所做的有没有一个术语？

00:13:50.590 --> 00:14:00.400
<v Man>Yeah, he's walking the walk. The area by the stadium or by the concert hall is called the walk. And then you're walking the walk. You're saying, "Who needs two?"
<v Man>有，他在“走秀”。体育场或音乐厅旁边的区域被称为“秀场”。你就是在“走秀”。你在说：“谁需要两张？”

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:02.270
<v Alex Blumberg>So he's just showed up here without any tickets.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以他就这样空着手出现在这儿了？

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:11.140
<v Man>Right. He's trying to buy some tickets, so then he can turn them around, and, we say "flip 'em," so he can flip  them for some dough, flip them for a profit.
<v Man>没错。他想买一些票，然后把它们转手卖掉，我们称之为“翻倍卖”，这样他就能赚点钱，翻倍赚取利润。

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.470
<v Leo>You got one extra? How much do you want for it? 20?
<v Leo>你有多余的一张吗？你想要多少？20？

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:16.330
<v Man>We're at row 60.
<v Man>我们是在第60排。

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.120
<v Leo>Row 60? How about 15?
<v Leo>第60排？15怎么样？

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.810
<v Man>How about 20?
<v Man>20怎么样？

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:20.430
<v Leo>20?
<v Leo>20？

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:20.850
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>是啊。

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:21.260
<v Leo>18?
<v Leo>18？

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.520
<v Man>$49 ticket.
<v Man>49美元的票。

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.890
<v Leo>OK. Sure you can't do 15?
<v Leo>好的。确定不能15吗？

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:29.210
<v Man>I'll do 18, but that's it.
<v Man>我可以做18，但就这么多。

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:30.160
<v Leo>There you go.
<v Leo>给你。

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.220
<v Man>Got it. Thanks.
<v Man>拿到了。谢谢。

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:32.660
<v Leo>Thanks.
<v Leo>谢谢。

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:43.060
<v Alex Blumberg>OK, so I actually timed this. From the moment he says "Thank you, man," to the moment he runs into another person is exactly 1 minute and 20 seconds.
<v Alex Blumberg>好了，我特意计时了。从他说“谢谢你，伙计”到他遇到下一个人，正好是1分20秒。

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:46.770
<v Leo>You guys need tickets? I got one. I'd sell it for 100.
<v Leo>你们需要票吗？我有一张。我卖100。

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.950
<v Man>You'd sell it for 100? Yeah, midfield.
<v Man>你卖100？是啊，中场位置。

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:49.880
<v Man>We ain't got that--
<v Man>我们没有那么多--

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.180
<v Alex Blumberg>Wait a minute. He just tried to sell it for $100?
<v Alex Blumberg>等一下。他刚才试着卖100美元？

00:14:53.180 --> 00:15:04.590
<v Man>You get what you can, you know? You might as well start high. You never know. Maybe the guy's going to pull out $100 and say, here. It can't hurt to start at $100 and come down.
<v Man>你能拿到多少就是多少，你知道吗？你最好一开始就报高价。谁知道呢。也许那家伙会掏出100美元说，给你。一开始报100美元再降价不会有什么坏处。

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:20.740
<v Man>You can't go up. You can't say $30, and if the guy says, "Only 30?" Be, "Oh, no, no, no. 50." Start as high-- you can always come down.
<v Man>你不能往上报。你不能报30美元，如果对方说，“才30？”然后说，“哦，不，不，不。50。”一开始就报高价——你总是可以降价的。

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:25.280
<v Chris>Did you have other techniques that you used when you were doing this?
<v Chris>当你做这个的时候，你还有其他使用的技巧吗？

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:51.370
<v Man>There's a whole psychology to what to do to sell someone tickets. Like when I was on the street, outside of the United Center, say, at a Bulls game, I'd present myself differently to different people, depending on what I thought they wanted out of this ticket-buying transaction. Some guys would be kind of nervous. And I'd see him walking up, and all the hustlers going up to him, trying to sell him tickets. And they'd look all wary. And they'd have a couple kids with them.
<v Man>卖票给别人有一整套心理学。比如当我在街上，在联合中心外面，比如在公牛队的比赛时，我会根据我认为他们想从这次购票交易中得到什么，而以不同的方式展现自己。有些人会很紧张。我看到他走过来，所有的拉皮条的人都围上去，试图卖票给他。他们看起来很警惕。他们还带着几个孩子。

00:15:51.370 --> 00:16:08.620
<v Man>So then I'd go up to them, and I'd just be like, "Sir, my uncle couldn't come to the game tonight. But I do have this extra ticket, if you guys want to come. They're great seats. I sit in them every game with my uncle." And I made him feel comfortable buying tickets from me.
<v Man>所以我走过去，我会说：“先生，我叔叔今晚不能来看比赛了。但我确实有一张多余的票，如果你们想来的话。位置很好。我每场比赛都和叔叔一起坐那儿。”我让他觉得从我这里买票很舒服。

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:27.870
<v Man>And sometimes, there were the guys who, they'd come with a few friends. And they wanted to show off that they could work with our underworld element, that they knew how to deal with scalpers. They'd be like, "I'll take care of it." They'd say to their friends, you'd see them, "I'll be right back." And they'd come up to me.
<v Man>有时候，还有些家伙和几个朋友一起来。他们想炫耀自己能和我们这些地下势力打交道，他们知道怎么应付黄牛。他们会说：“这事儿我搞定。”你会看到他们对朋友说，“我一会儿就回来。”然后他们会走过来找我。

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:48.570
<v Man>And then I'd be the hardened street hustler. And I'd be looking around constantly. I'd be saying, "Come on, man. The heat's tight out here tonight. Work with me. Work with me. What are you payin'? What are you payin'? What do you need?" Just trying to make them feel like they were really wheeling and dealing.
<v Man>然后我会扮演强硬的街头混混。我会不断地环顾四周。我会说：“快点，伙计。今晚管得严。跟我配合好。跟我配合好。你付多少？你付多少？你需要什么？”只是想让他们觉得他们真的在进行一场重大交易。

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:57.830
<v Alex Blumberg>Did you always win? Did the customer ever have a chance?
<v Alex Blumberg>你总是赢吗？顾客有机会吗？

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:25.750
<v Man>Really, out of every transaction, both people win. If they paid you it, then they could afford it. So many people would buy tickets from me at just what I imagine to be an exorbitant-- I myself was like, this price is ridiculous. And yet, they would be so happy. They would be glowing. And they were so appreciative and thankful to me, for getting them into the game.
<v Man>说实话，每一笔交易，双方都是赢家。如果他们付了钱，说明他们负担得起。很多人以我看来极其昂贵的价格从我这儿买票——我自己都觉得这价格简直荒谬。然而，他们会非常开心。他们会容光焕发。他们非常感激我能让他们进入球场。

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:49.160
<v Man>But I did make it a rule, sometimes a point of pride, to not let them go in with any, you know, to see how much money they had in their wallet. And I was going to take all of it. They didn't need popcorn in the game. They didn't need a beer.
<v Man>但我确实制定了一条规则，有时是一种自尊心，不让他们进去时口袋里还剩什么钱。我要把钱全拿走。他们在球场里不需要爆米花，也不需要啤酒。

00:17:49.160 --> 00:18:21.460
<v Man>Being a ticket broker is different than being in other businesses, where if you run a bar, and you run out of Bud Lite, you call up and have them pull that truck right up out back in the loading dock and wheel about 20 cases off. But when you want to get tickets, it's not that simple. There's no easy way to do it. So ticket brokers have to rely on just a whole network of shadowy associates to bring the tickets into them.
<v Man>做票务经纪人不同于其他生意。如果你开了一家酒吧，百威淡啤卖完了，你可以打个电话让卡车开到卸货平台，卸下20箱。但当你想要票时，没那么简单。没有简单的方法。所以票务经纪人必须依靠一整套阴影里的关系网把票搞到手。

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:47.540
<v Man>There's a guy named Moose who has a whole bike gang. And every year, when Cubs games would go on sale, at the Cubs on sale, you're only allowed to buy eight tickets for up to eight games, so 64 tickets total. He would get about 15 of his biker friends. He would just keep them up all night on Friday night drinking, merrymaking, singing, whatever it would take.
<v Man>有个叫穆斯的家伙，他有一个完整的摩托帮。每年，当小熊队门票开售时，小熊队规定每人最多只能买八场比赛的八张票，总共64张。他会找大约15个骑摩托车的朋友。他会在周五晚上整晚让他们喝酒、狂欢、唱歌，不管用什么手段。

00:18:47.540 --> 00:19:02.230
<v Man>So when the tickets went on sale, he'd then have 15 guys who were waiting there. And hopefully the weren't passed out. They were waiting there to buy 64 tickets each. When he brings them in to the ticket broker, the broker will pay him $10 over the face value for each ticket.
<v Man>所以当票开售时，他就会有15个人在那儿等着。希望他们没喝醉。他们在那里等着每人买64张票。当他把票带给票务经纪人时，经纪人会为每张票支付比面值高出10美元的价格。

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:18.490
<v Man>At the Bulls on sales, they started doing this lottery system. They didn't want people to just be camping out two weeks beforehand, because they knew that these tickets were going to be worth so much. So a couple enterprising ticket brokers started rounding up the homeless guys around Chicago.
<v Man>在公牛队开售时，他们开始实行这种抽签系统。他们不想让人在两周前就露宿街头，因为他们知道这些票价值连城。于是几个投机取巧的票务经纪人开始在芝加哥周围搜罗无家可归者。

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:39.450
<v Man>The actually would rent a school bus, and they would drive around Chicago, just pulling people into the bus, promising them $10 or $20 if they'd come hang out for the morning. Sometimes they'd even pull up in front of the homeless shelter right down there on South Wabash or South State and just toot the horn. And all the guys would come filing out and fill up the bus.
<v Man>他们实际上会租一辆校车，然后在芝加哥到处开，把人拉上车，承诺给他们10美元或20美元，让他们在早上过来排队。有时候他们甚至会停在南沃巴什或南州街的流浪汉收容所门前，按响喇叭。然后所有人都会排队出来，坐满那辆车。

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:59.380
<v Man>They'd drive them down to the United Center box office window. When the tickets went on sale, you'd have, out of the first 60 people in line, there'd be 50 homeless guys waiting to buy these $100 tickets. And the brokers would just give them a few hundred bucks each to buy the tickets they wanted.
<v Man>他们把他们拉到联合中心的售票窗口。当票开售时，排在前60个人里，会有50个流浪汉等着买这些100美元的门票。经纪人只会给他们每人几百美元，让他们买他们想要的票。

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:13.370
<v Man>Every once in a while, tickets would be released into the system. Suddenly they would become available. A game that had been sold out, suddenly there are six tickets that are available. And maybe it's a season ticket holder who has canceled, and said, "I can't come to this game."
<v Man>每隔一段时间，门票会被释放进系统。突然之间它们就可以买了。一场本来已经售罄的比赛，突然有六张票可以买了。也许是一个季票持有者取消了，说：“我不能来看这场比赛了。”

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:29.280
<v Man>Or David Stern, the NBA commissioner, he actually has four front row seats to every game in the NBA, every night. And there's no way that he can go to every game every night. In fact, a lot of nights he doesn't go to any game.
<v Man>或者大卫·斯特恩，NBA总裁，他实际上每场比赛都有四个前排座位，每晚都有。他不可能每晚去每场比赛。事实上，很多晚上他一场比赛都不去。

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:45.480
<v Man>So sometime the day of the game, they'll release those seats. And those four beautiful seats will suddenly become available into the system. So whoever's the first person to phone in, go to a window, or punch in online at that precise second is going to be the one that gets them.
<v Man>所以比赛当天的某个时候，他们会释放这些座位。那四个漂亮的座位突然就会出现在系统中。所以谁是第一个打电话、走到窗口或在那一秒钟准时在网上预订的人，谁就能得到它们。

00:20:45.480 --> 00:21:33.030
<v Man>So Monet, Monet Joe, what he would do is all night, he would just hang out at his computer, keep pecking, pecking, pecking away, with the Grateful Dead playing and smoking a J and just pecking away, pecking away. Usually it says, "Sorry, your selection is not available." He would peck again, one ticket. He might peck 1,000 times. But then all of a sudden, your screen lights up and gives you that section and row number and says, "Would you like to finish this purchase?" And you know you just made-- you can buy that ticket for face value, which is about as low as you can buy a ticket, and sell it for 10 times that, sometimes.
<v Man>所以莫奈，莫奈·乔，他会怎么做呢，整晚他都会待在他的电脑前，不停地敲击，伴着感恩而死乐队的音乐，抽着大麻，不停地敲击。通常上面写着：“对不起，您选择的座位不可用。”他会再敲一次，买一张票。他可能会敲1000次。但突然之间，你的屏幕亮了起来，给了你那个区域和排数，并说：“你想完成这次购买吗？”你知道你刚刚……你可以按面值买到那张票，那是你能买到的最低价格，有时可以以10倍的价格卖出。

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:40.360
<v Alex Blumberg>During the time that you worked, during those five years that you worked as a scalper, how was the money?
<v Alex Blumberg>在你工作的那五年里，也就是你做黄牛的那段时间，收入如何？

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:51.340
<v Man>It was pretty phat. It was sick. It was humongous. I had a suitcase of money.
<v Man>相当丰厚。简直疯了。太庞大了。我有一个装满钱的行李箱。

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.330
<v Alex Blumberg>You had a literal suitcase of money?
<v Alex Blumberg>你有一个名副其实的装满钱的行李箱？

00:21:53.330 --> 00:22:19.050
<v Man>Yeah. It was actually so much money that I didn't want to deposit it at one time in a bank. I didn't want to freak out the bank manager or anything like that. So I would put a little bit in one bank, a little bit in another bank. And then there was a third bank where I had a safety deposit box. And I would just stuff the cash into the safety deposit box.
<v Man>是啊。其实钱多到我不想一次存进银行。我不想吓坏银行经理之类的。所以我会在一家银行存一点，在另一家银行存一点。还有第三家银行，我有一个保险箱。我就把现金塞进保险箱里。

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:45.270
<v Man>The Bulls days, Michael Jordan, were really the golden age of tickets in Chicago. There's always peaks and valleys. And I'm sure Yankees tickets are pretty nice now. Laker tickets are alright. But it was a rare, it wasn't just one sports team having a nice run. It really became something that-- it became the focus of the entire world.
<v Man>公牛队的时代，迈克尔·乔丹，真的是芝加哥门票的黄金时代。总是有高峰和低谷。我相信洋基队的票现在也不错。湖人队的票也还可以。但这很罕见，不仅仅是一支运动队表现好。它真的变成了……它成为了全世界关注的焦点。

00:22:45.270 --> 00:23:07.150
<v Man>You'd meet people from Sweden and Japan and Korea and England and Germany, and you were like a celebrity to them. You had what everybody else in the world wanted. You had a stack of tickets to the Chicago Bulls game. And people would do anything for them, and they would pay any price.
<v Man>你会见到来自瑞典、日本、韩国、英国和德国的人，对他们来说你就像名人。你拥有世界上其他人想要的东西。你有一叠芝加哥公牛队比赛的门票。人们为了这些票什么都愿意做，而且他们会支付任何价格。

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:34.830
<v Man>We felt like we were part of something bigger than us. We felt like we were part of this historical moment. The day Michael Jordan retired in 1998, in June, that's the same day I retired.
<v Man>我们感觉我们是比我们自身更宏大的一部分。我们感觉我们是这历史时刻的一部分。1998年6月迈克尔·乔丹退役的那天，也是我退役的日子。

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:51.800
<v Man>Michael Jordan probably sits around with his cousins and friends and talked about his great shots that he made, his game-winning dunks. Some of the ticket people sit around and talk about the great sales that they made for Bulls games. Like, I talked to my friend, John, and he's got a story about it.
<v Man>迈克尔·乔丹可能和他的表兄弟和朋友坐在一起，谈论他投出的伟大进球，他比赛中的绝杀扣篮。一些卖票的人坐在一起谈论他们在公牛队比赛中完成的伟大交易。比如，我和我的朋友约翰谈过，他也有关于这方面的故事。

00:23:51.800 --> 00:24:03.200
<v John>An old lady was walking down the street. She passed all the hustlers up. And nobody had thought to ask her. But I was walking down the street, and I just said, "Do anybody have any extra tickets?"
<v John>一位老太太走在街上。她经过了所有的拉皮条的人。没人想到要去问她。但我当时走在街上，我只是说：“有人有多余的票吗？”

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:13.870
<v John>She said, "Do you need tickets?" I said, "Yeah." And she said, "Yeah, I got a couple of extra tickets, section 111, row 15."
<v John>她说：“你需要票吗？”我说：“是的。”她说：“是的，我有两张多余的票，111区，第15排。”

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:28.740
<v John>These was the best tickets. These was in center court, 15 rows from the floor. She sold me the tickets at $85 each, which at that time, was face value tickets. I, in turn, didn't even get a chance to walk 10 feet away after I bought the tickets.
<v John>这是最好的票。这是中场，距离场地15排。她以每张85美元的价格把票卖给了我，在当时，那是面值票。我呢，甚至还没来得及走开10英尺，在我买票之后。

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:48.100
<v John>A whole gang of people got around me. And everybody was bidding on them. When we got finished bidding with those tickets, those tickets sold at $1,200 cash a ticket.
<v John>一大群人围住了我。每个人都在竞价。当我们竞价结束时，那些票以每张1200美元的现金卖出。

00:24:48.100 --> 00:25:05.010
<v John>I was so happy. And the guy that bought them was a Chinese man. Him and his wife went. They thought I was God, because they had the best seats. And I stuffed the money in my pocket and skipped on down the street.
<v John>我太高兴了。买下它们的那个人是个中国人。他和他的妻子去了。他们觉得我简直是上帝，因为他们得到了最好的座位。我把钱塞进口袋，一路跳着走下街道。

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:24.710
<v John>But that was the most memorable thing. This is what a hustler dream of, of a deal like that, because you hardly ever find a deal like that. You can hardly ever find tickets on a Jordan day. Even if you do a ticket like that, you're going to pay a few hundred dollars for it, and then you're going to run around and try to sell it for big money.
<v John>但那是印象最深刻的事情。这就是拉皮条的人梦寐以求的交易，因为你很难遇到那样的交易。在乔丹时代，你几乎找不到票。即使你真的拿到一张那样的票，你也得支付几百美元，然后你要到处跑，试图以大价钱卖掉它。

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:43.740
<v John>But to get a deal like that, it just stays with you. There's nothing to say about a deal like that. Bought cheap, sold high, what more can I say?
<v John>但能做成那样一笔交易，它会一直留在你记忆里。没什么好说的，那样的交易。低买高卖，我还能说什么呢？

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:53.630
<v John>Yeah, well, it's just not like it used to be during the Jordan era. What can I say? It's terrible. It is. It's bad.
<v John>是啊，嗯，现在不像乔丹时代那样了。我还能说什么？太糟糕了。就是这样。太差了。

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:55.420
<v Man>How bad is it?
<v Man>有多差？

00:25:55.420 --> 00:26:27.490
<v John>Here, I'll show you. One day, I was at the White Sox, trying to sell tickets, right? And I had this guy that was going to buy a couple tickets from me. And wasn't charging a lot. And it was a $27 ticket. I was going to give it to him for $20 apiece. And a fan walked up and just gave him two tickets. That's how bad it was. And the guy said, "Thanks for talking to me," and went to the ballpark.
<v John>给你，我给你看。有一天，我在白袜队比赛现场，试图卖票，对吧？有个家伙要从我这儿买两张票。我没收很多钱。那是一张27美元的票。我本来要以每张20美元的价格卖给他。结果一个粉丝走过来直接给了他两张票。情况就是这么糟糕。那家伙说：“谢谢你跟我说话，”然后就去了球场。

00:26:27.490 --> 00:27:07.160
<v Man>It's a cold, dark, rainy, miserable, February day every day. And it's [INAUDIBLE] now. Where instead of the gleaming bright sun of the Bulls era. It used to be this page had about 30, 40 ads every day in here. Now there's, what, one, two, three, four, five, six. So a lot of people are out of the game that once were in it. It wasn't just me that retired.
<v Man>每一天都是寒冷、阴暗、潮湿、悲惨的二月天。现在就是这样。而公牛时代则有着闪耀的太阳。以前这张页面每天大约有30、40条广告。现在呢，只有一、二、三、四、五、六条。所以很多人已经退出了这个游戏。不仅仅是我退役了。

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:09.380
<v Ira Glass>Our ticket scalper talked with This American Life producer Alex Blumberg.
<v Ira Glass>我们的票贩子与《美国生活》的制作人亚历克斯·布隆伯格进行了交谈。

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:44.780
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "TWO TICKETS TO PARADISE" BY EDDIE MONEY]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 埃迪·莫内的《通往天堂的两张票》]

00:27:44.780 --> 00:28:56.970
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, 60,000 Memphis fans can't be wrong about you, can they? In a minute from Public Radio International and Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，6万名孟菲斯粉丝不可能看错你们，是吧？从公共广播国际和芝加哥公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:28:56.970 --> 00:29:12.740
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Middlemen, in which we examine what it means to be the monkey in the middle. We have arrived at Act Two of our program, Act Two, Stuck Inside of Memphis.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目：中间人，我们将探讨当“中间猴子”意味着什么。我们已经进入了节目的第二幕，第二幕，困在孟菲斯。

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:35.140
<v Ira Glass>A while back, there was a fire in Beth Landau's apartment building in New York. And when the firemen from around the corner were responding to the call, she and her mom, who was visiting, got to talking with one of them, a guy named Sal Princiatta. Sal was a huge Elvis fan. He looks a bit like Elvis. And as fate had it, Beth's mom went to high school in Memphis with Elvis. So there was a lot to talk about.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，贝丝·兰道在纽约的公寓楼里发生了火灾。当附近街区的消防队员赶来响应呼救时，她和正在探访的母亲开始与其中一名叫萨尔·普林西亚塔的队员交谈。萨尔是埃迪·普雷斯利的超级粉丝。他长得有点像埃迪。巧合的是，贝丝的妈妈在孟菲斯和埃迪上同一所高中。所以他们有很多话可以聊。

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:52.170
<v Ira Glass>OK, time passes. After September 11th, Beth checks at the firehouse around the corner to see if Sal is OK. This is one of the units that lost a lot of guys. And it turns out that he survived. He's depressed. Like a lot of firefighters, he developed asthma from digging in the dust and the rubble in the weeks after September 11th, but he is alive.
<v Ira Glass>好了，时间过去了。9月11日之后，贝丝到附近街区的消防站查看萨尔是否还好。这是损失惨重的单位之一。结果他活了下来。他很沮丧。像许多消防员一样，他在9月11日之后的几周里因为挖掘灰尘和瓦砾而患上了哮喘，但他还活着。

00:29:52.170 --> 00:30:30.570
<v Ira Glass>She starts dropping by with food for the guys, hanging out. And wanting to do something nice for Sal, she organizes a trip to Memphis for him, to see Graceland. Other firemen from the house get interested in going. And in the end, in May of 2002, 18 of them went as guests of the Memphis Fire Department and many other sponsors, with stops at Al Green's church, all the varied requisite Elvis sights, and an appearance before 60,000 people at the Sunset Symphony on the banks of the Mississippi. And Sal found that for the people of Memphis who they met, he and the other guys were a very particular kind of middleman, a very unusual kind of middleman.
<v Ira Glass>她开始带着食物去探望队员们，在那儿待着。为了给萨尔做点好事，她组织了一次孟菲斯之行，让他去参观雅园。消防站的其他消防员也对此产生了兴趣。最终，在2002年5月，他们18人作为孟菲斯消防局和其他许多赞助商的客人前往孟菲斯，期间参观了阿尔·格林的教堂，所有必需的埃迪相关景点，并在密西西比河畔的日落交响音乐会上出现在6万名观众面前。萨尔发现，对于他们遇到的孟菲斯民众来说，他和队员们成了某种非常特殊的中间人，一种非常不寻常的中间人。

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:42.780
<v Sal Princiatta>When we arrived into Memphis, as soon as they find out who you are, you're a rock star.
<v Sal Princiatta>当我们到达孟菲斯时，他们一发现你是谁，你就是摇滚明星。

00:30:42.780 --> 00:31:04.720
<v Man>And now for what you have all been waiting for. We're here to meet the heroes from New York City. These guys are from Engine House 33, Ladder Company 9, which I think is about a mile from the World Trade Center. These were some first guys on the scene, and they lost 10 men out of their 40-man crew. So these are the real heroes of New York City.
<v Man>现在，为您带来大家期待已久的人物。我们在这里迎接来自纽约市的英雄。这些人来自第33发动机房，第9云梯连，我想这离世贸中心大约一英里。他们是现场的第一批救援人员，40人的团队里牺牲了10人。所以这些是真正的纽约市英雄。

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:07.270
<v Sal Princiatta>We represent good, you know.
<v Sal Princiatta>我们代表美好，你知道。

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:12.590
<v Man>Sal, I'd like for Sal "Elvis" Princiatta to come up.
<v Man>萨尔，我想请萨尔“埃迪”普林西亚塔上来。

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:29.270
<v Sal Princiatta>Everybody wishes they could have been there, I think, in one way, for one hour, for one minute. So we connect them to that one minute. It's unnerving. And a lot of the guys feel the same way.
<v Sal Princiatta>我想，每个人都希望自己能在那儿，在某种意义上，哪怕是一个小时，一分钟。所以我们把他们与那一分钟联系起来。这让人不安。许多队员也有同样的感觉。

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:37.530
<v Sal Princiatta>It's very unnerving to be applauded. I just can't take it. I appreciate it, but it's unnerving. It's embarrassing.
<v Sal Princiatta>被鼓掌让人非常不安。我真的受不了。我很感激，但这让人不安。这让人尴尬。

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:48.510
<v Sal Princiatta>We represent, we are FDNY, and that's what they see. They don't see names, not me, not Pete, not Joe, not anybody. They see what we do.
<v Sal Princiatta>我们代表，我们是FDNY，这就是他们看到的。他们看不到名字，不是我，不是皮特，不是乔，不是任何人。他们看到的是我们所做的事。

00:31:48.510 --> 00:32:07.580
<v Sal Princiatta>We see, and we know, that we pretty much didn't do anything. I think they feel sorry for us, because we lost the battle, maybe almost pity us. They do pity us. That's pity out there. That's pity treatment.
<v Sal Princiatta>我们看到，并且我们知道，我们其实什么都没做。我想他们为我们感到难过，因为我们输掉了那场战斗，也许近乎怜悯我们。他们确实怜悯我们。那是怜悯。那是怜悯的对待。

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:12.300
<v Boy>Are y'all friends of those people that died in the World Trade Center?
<v Boy>你们是那些在世贸中心遇难的人的朋友吗？

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:17.780
<v Firefighter>Mm-hmm.
<v Firefighter>嗯。

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:31.250
<v Woman>I welcome you to Sun Studios, the birthplace of rock and roll. And if you have any questions, hold them until the end of the tour. And if you have any pagers or cell  phones, then turn those off now, because we are entering the year 1950.
<v Woman>欢迎来到太阳录音室，摇滚乐的发源地。如果您有任何问题，请等到参观结束时再提。如果您有任何寻呼机或手机，现在请把它们关掉，因为我们正在进入1950年。

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:39.730
<v Sal Princiatta>Sun Records was great. The birth of rock and roll, that was a good place to be. That's one part that sticks out in my head a lot.
<v Sal Princiatta>太阳唱片公司太棒了。摇滚乐的诞生，那是个好地方。那是我脑海中挥之不去的一部分。

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:43.500
<v Man>Everybody that comes off the tour, I'm going to give y'all one of our short-sleeve t-shirts over here.
<v Man>每一个参观结束出来的人，我都要送你们一件我们的短袖T恤。

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:45.450
<v Woman>No way, they're getting a short-sleeve t-shirt?
<v Woman>不会吧，他们要送短袖T恤？

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:55.970
<v Man>Black, white, burgundy, the blue, also in black, and all different style t-shirts. Perfect. Just pick your size, my friend.
<v Man>黑色、白色、勃艮第红、蓝色，还有黑色，以及各种不同风格的T恤。太完美了。选好你的尺码，我的朋友。

00:32:55.970 --> 00:33:03.700
<v Sal Princiatta>So to take gifts from people, how do I feel? People want to pacify, and want to do what they can.
<v Sal Princiatta>所以接受人们的礼物，我感觉如何？人们想要安慰，想要尽他们所能。

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:15.370
<v Woman>And so, I present a pair of gold and clausinate cuff links with the city seal.
<v Woman>于是，我送上一对带有城市印章的金色袖扣。

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:27.100
<v Sal Princiatta>But it's hard to conceive. You're getting gifts while your friends are dead. That's all. How does that equate? What's the purpose?
<v Sal Princiatta>但很难理解。你的朋友们都死了，你却在收礼物。就是这样。这怎么能等同呢？目的是什么？

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:31.240
<v Man>These are real heroes. These guys are from New York, World Trade Center.
<v Man>这些是真正的英雄。这些人来自纽约，世贸中心。

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:32.650
<v Firefighter>What's up guys, how are you?
<v Firefighter>怎么了，伙计们，你们好吗？

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:48.430
<v Sal Princiatta>I've seen a cartoon in the newspaper of Spider-man, Batman, and Superman asking for a firefighter's autograph. And that's just too much to be put on any average joe's back. Because that's really what we are.
<v Sal Princiatta>我在报纸上看到过一幅漫画，蜘蛛侠、蝙蝠侠和超人在向消防员要签名。这对于任何普通人来说都太沉重了。因为我们真的就是普通人。

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:54.700
<v Man>And these great guys are here for three days. Let's give them a standing Memphis ovation.
<v Man>这些伟大的伙计们在这里待了三天。让我们为他们送上孟菲斯的起立鼓掌。

00:33:54.700 --> 00:34:07.630
<v Sal Princiatta>The symphony was pretty cool. I don't really like speaking in public. We were there for the closing ceremonies of Memphis in May. Or was it May in Memphis? I can't remember.
<v Sal Princiatta>交响乐相当不错。我不太喜欢在公开场合发言。我们在那里参加孟菲斯五月节的闭幕式。还是五月的孟菲斯？我不记得了。

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:20.190
<v Sal Princiatta>A lot of people get emotional. Maybe they see the pain in our eyes, and for anybody who was ever a soldier, you could tell. They see the look in the eyes. So they feel for us.
<v Sal Princiatta>很多人会情绪激动。也许他们看到了我们眼中的痛苦，对于任何当过兵的人来说，你就能看出来。他们看到了那样的眼神。所以他们为我们感到难过。

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:33.090
<v Sal Princiatta>And also, it's their own grief, I would think, that they're letting out. They can't say enough. So they repeat themselves, which is fine.
<v Sal Princiatta>此外，我想他们是在释放自己的悲伤。他们说不完。所以他们反复重复，这没关系。

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:37.410
<v Man>I'd like to just shake your hand, bud. Y'all did a hell of a job. And I would
<v Man>我只想和你握个手，伙计。你们做得很棒。而且我想要

