<v Sal Princiatta>[咳嗽声]

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:24.880
<v Sal Princiatta>I was having a hard time. I dug there a lot of days. That's why my health is so bad. But I was having a real hard time, until I got warmed up.
<v Sal Princiatta>我当时很难受。我在那儿挖了很多天。这就是为什么我的健康状况这么糟。但在我热身之前，我真的很难受。

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:46.630
<v Sal Princiatta>If we had our team, if we had our A-team, we would have definitely beat them. Like we have Mike and Dave, who were killed in 9/11. They were great outfielders. If they were there, it's a different game. If Gerarde Baptiste, that's three outfielders. If we had those guys there, we would have picked them apart.
<v Sal Princiatta>如果我们有我们的团队，如果我们有我们的王牌队伍，我们肯定会打败他们。比如我们有迈克和戴夫，他们在9/11事件中牺牲了。他们是伟大的外场手。如果他们在，比赛结果就不一样了。如果还有杰拉德·巴蒂斯特，那就是三个外场手。如果我们在那儿有那些伙计，我们会把他们彻底击败的。

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:58.190
<v Sal Princiatta>We lost a lot of guys from the team. And in fact, a recent guy, Gary Guidell, who just took his own life, was on the team. And he was a good guy, Gary.
<v Sal Princiatta>我们损失了队里的很多伙计。事实上，最近有一个队员，加里·吉德尔，刚刚自杀了，他也在队里。他是个好人，加里。

00:36:58.190 --> 00:37:29.460
<v Sal Princiatta>So I think that's another reason why guys are feeling a little bit vulnerable at this point. Because Gary was a regular joe. He didn't show any signs of hurting any more than any one of us. So that stirred up a lot of fright in a lot of-- in myself. I can speak for myself, but I'm sure I can speak for others, that what happened to Gary is everybody's biggest fear right now, to take your own life.
<v Sal Princiatta>所以我认为这是队员们此刻感到脆弱的另一个原因。因为加里是个普通人。他没有表现出比我们任何人都更痛苦的迹象。所以这在很多人——我自己心中引起了很多恐惧。我可以代表我自己，但我确信我也可以代表其他人，加里身上发生的事情是每个人现在最大的恐惧，那就是自杀。

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:35.690
<v Woman>Thank you so much for allowing us to be photographed. You make us very proud.
<v Woman>非常感谢你们允许我们拍照。你们让我们感到非常骄傲。

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:37.310
<v Firefighter>Thank you.
<v Firefighter>谢谢。

00:37:37.310 --> 00:37:40.350
<v Man>Guys, let's go-- you guys have signed the paper, right?
<v Man>伙计们，我们走吧——你们签了文件对吧？

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:42.380
<v Firefighter>You got it.
<v Firefighter>没问题。

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:49.340
<v Ira Glass>Sal Princiatta. His story was produced with Beth Landau, David van Taylor, and Allie Pomeroy at Lumiere Productions in New York.
<v Ira Glass>萨尔·普林西亚塔。他的故事是由贝丝·兰道、大卫·范·泰勒和艾莉·波梅罗在纽约Lumiere Productions制作的。

00:37:49.340 --> 00:38:28.410
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "LONELY MAN" BY ELVIS PRESLEY]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 埃迪·普雷斯利的《孤独的人》]

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:48.610
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, What It Takes to Tromp Through the Desert. So Moses was this great leader who brought the Israelites out of Egypt. One of the things the Bible says about him is that he was not much of a talker. Whenever he had to give a big speech or tell Pharaoh to let his people go, he would bring along his brother, Aaron, to do the talking for him. This is the story of somebody who's something like that.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，在沙漠中跋涉需要什么。摩西是带领以色列人走出埃及的伟大领袖。圣经中关于他的一点是，他不善言辞。每当他需要发表重要演讲或告诉法老让他的子民离开时，他都会带上他的兄弟亚伦代为发言。这就是一个像那样的人的故事。

00:38:48.610 --> 00:39:05.640
<v Ira Glass>Al Jurczynski is the middleman who is leading hundreds of people who live in New York City to the promised land of Schenectady, New York. And as he would tell you himself, what makes him good at his job is, he is just a normal guy. No flashy speeches. You aren't dazzled by charisma when you meet him. But you trust him.
<v Ira Glass>阿尔·朱琴斯基是一个中间人，他正在带领成百上千住在纽约市的人前往纽约州斯克内克塔迪的应许之地。正如他自己会告诉你的那样，他擅长这份工作的原因是，他只是个普通人。没有华丽的演讲。当你见到他时，你不会被他的魅力所倾倒。但你信任他。

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:26.200
<v Ira Glass>Enough people trust him that he's accomplishing a very unlikely mission. Most of the people who he's convinced to move from the Bronx and Queens are people who immigrated there from the South American country of Guyana. These are people whose ancestors mostly came from India and Africa, moving to an old Rust Belt town of white ethnics. Our producer, Wendy Dorr, went to watch him work.
<v Ira Glass>足够多的人信任他，以至于他正在完成一项非常不可能完成的任务。他劝说从布朗克斯区和皇后区搬离的大多数人，都是从南美国家圭亚那移民到那里的人。这些人是祖先大多来自印度和非洲的人，搬到了一个白人聚集的老锈带城镇。我们的制作人温迪·多尔去观察了他的工作。

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:54.050
<v Wendy Dorr>Mayor Jurczynski is the sort of politician who's great at talking with old ladies or small businessmen, or really with anybody, for about a minute and a half. But after that, after his opening lines, he goes into this patter. If you hang around him for more than a few hours, you hear the same lines over and over. There are awkward jokes. But he's making such an effort that you kind of forgive him anything. Here he is, showing a busload of Guyanese New Yorkers around Schenectady.
<v Wendy Dorr>朱琴斯基市长属于那种擅长与老太太或小商贩，或者真的与任何人交谈大约一分半钟的政客。但在那之后，在他开场白之后，他就会进入这种固定的套路。如果你在他身边待超过几个小时，你会听到同样的话一遍又一遍。还有一些尴尬的笑话。但他做出如此努力，以至于你几乎会原谅他所做的一切。这是他带领一车圭亚那裔纽约人参观斯克内克塔迪的情景。

00:39:54.050 --> 00:40:20.760
<v Al Jurczynski>But you know, there's people, Guyanese people, that have come here. And they said, "I've been to Schenectady. It's a little too slow for me. I like the fast pace of New York City." So that's understandable. And I like to tell people that come up here on the bus that if you like traffic jams, you should stay in New York City.
<v Al Jurczynski>但你知道，有些人，圭亚那人，来到这里。他们说，“我去过斯克内克塔迪。对我来说太慢了。我喜欢纽约市的快节奏。”所以这是可以理解的。我喜欢告诉那些坐车上来的人，如果你喜欢堵车，你就应该待在纽约市。

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:23.040
<v Al Jurczynski>That was a joke. You know, my jokes are getting pretty old.
<v Al Jurczynski>那是个笑话。你知道，我的笑话越来越老套了。

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:31.020
<v Al Jurczynski>[LAUGHTER]
<v Al Jurczynski>[笑声]

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:39.500
<v Al Jurczynski>I know one thing. Whenever I run out of things to say when I'm talking to Guyanese people, all I got to do is mention cricket. And then everybody starts talking.
<v Al Jurczynski>我知道一件事。每当我和圭亚那人交谈时没话找话，我只要提一下板球就行了。然后每个人都会开始交谈。

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:44.620
<v Wendy Dorr>He looks around the bus. Nobody starts talking.
<v Wendy Dorr>他环顾车内。没人开始说话。

00:40:44.620 --> 00:41:01.410
<v Wendy Dorr>In 1995, when he first ran for mayor, Schenectady was going through the same kinds of pains as lots of Rust Belt cities. The biggest employer, General Electric, had downsized from 40,000 employees to just a few thousand. Tens of thousands of people had moved away, or were forced to retire.
<v Wendy Dorr>1995年，当他第一次竞选市长时，斯克内克塔迪正经历着许多锈带城市同样的痛苦。最大的雇主通用电气公司已经从4万名员工裁员到只剩下几千人。成千上万的人搬走了，或者被迫退休。

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:22.540
<v Wendy Dorr>For a while, Al Jurczynski had this idea to lure Hasidic Jews to Schenectady. He wanted to repopulate certain problem neighborhoods with a group that was self-reliant, family-oriented, and tightly knit. That idea went nowhere. But then last year, some Guyanese people came to him for help getting an abandoned church for religious services.
<v Wendy Dorr>有一段时间，阿尔·朱琴斯基想出一个主意，引诱哈西德犹太人搬到斯克内克塔迪。他想用一个自力更生、以家庭为中心、紧密团结的群体来重新填充某些有问题的小区。这个想法没成功。但去年，一些圭亚那人来找他帮忙，想弄到一个废弃的教堂用于宗教服务。

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:40.140
<v Al Jurczynski>Well, we talked. And I talked about public assistance. And we had had quite a problem with a lot of people coming up from New York City that were not coming up here to work, but were getting on public assistance. A lot of them were doing things that we didn't want them to do, namely get involved in the drug trade.
<v Al Jurczynski>嗯，我们谈了谈。我谈到了公共援助。我们有很多从纽约市上来的人，他们不是来这里工作的，而是来领取公共救济金的。他们中的很多人在做我们不希望他们做的事，即卷入毒品交易。

00:41:40.140 --> 00:42:20.460
<v Al Jurczynski>And they made it very clear, right up front, within probably the first 5 or 10 minutes that I met with them, that they don't believe in public assistance unless it's absolutely necessary. But it's against their culture to get on public assistance. And I heard that. I was extremely impressed by that. It was just so wonderful and so refreshing. And at that point, that's when I really I started to really embrace them.
<v Al Jurczynski>他们很明确地表达了立场，在我见到他们的前5到10分钟内，他们不相信公共援助，除非万不得已。但领取公共援助违背了他们的文化。我听进去了。我对此印象非常深刻。这真是太棒了，太让人耳目一新了。就在那时，我开始真正接纳他们。

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:28.360
<v Herman Singh>Thanks, folks, you're in tune to Herman Singh Showtime. It's 93.5 FM on your dial. It's now 20 minutes before 11:00 AM.
<v Herman Singh>感谢各位，你们正在收听赫尔曼·辛格的节目。拨号盘上的93.5兆赫。现在是11点前20分。

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:54.020
<v Wendy Dorr>And that's where Herman Singh comes in. He's a realtor slash mortgage broker slash weekly radio host down in New York City, in the Queens neighborhood where most Guyanese live. It was his bright idea to bus Guyanese up to Schenectady every Saturday, to encourage them to buy houses up there. He pays for the buses himself, and promotes the trip like crazy on his weekly real estate slash music program, Herman Singh Showtime.
<v Wendy Dorr>这就是赫尔曼·辛格介入的地方。他是房地产经纪人兼抵押贷款经纪人兼每周电台主持人，在大多数圭亚那人居住的皇后区。他有个好主意，每周六用巴士把圭亚那人送到斯克内克塔迪，鼓励他们在那儿买房子。他自己掏钱支付巴士费用，并在这个名为《赫尔曼·辛格秀》的每周房地产兼音乐节目中疯狂宣传这次旅行。

00:42:54.020 --> 00:43:02.040
<v Herman Singh>If you're planning to purchase a property in Schenectady, our next trip is actually going up to Schenectady on Saturday. And room with friends, if you are selling your property--
<v Herman Singh>如果您正计划在斯克内克塔迪购买房产，我们下一次行程实际上是在周六去斯克内克塔迪。和朋友住在一起，如果您要出售您的房产——

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:14.220
<v Wendy Dorr>At the beginning of the mayor's initiative to bring Guyanese to Schenectady five months ago, the mayor went on Herman's radio show and gave out his personal cell phone number on the air, a political gesture he explains this way.
<v Wendy Dorr>在五个月前市长启动引进圭亚那人到斯克内克塔迪的计划之初，市长上了赫尔曼的电台节目，并在广播中公布了他的个人手机号码，这是一种政治姿态，他这样解释。

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:26.530
<v Al Jurczynski>I have free incoming calls. So if I was paying for the incoming calls, I wouldn't have done it. And I'd do it again.
<v Al Jurczynski>我有免费的入站电话。所以如果我要支付入站电话费，我就不会这么做了。我会再做一次。

00:43:26.530 --> 00:43:29.270
<v Wendy Dorr>He gives me a sheepish look, and then blurts--
<v Wendy Dorr>他羞涩地看了我一眼，然后脱口而出——

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:42.540
<v Al Jurczynski>857-4000, area code 518. If you want to buy a home in Schenectady, give me a call.
<v Al Jurczynski>857-4000，区号518。如果你想在斯克内克塔迪买房，给我打个电话。

00:43:42.540 --> 00:44:04.440
<v Wendy Dorr>One Saturday in December, I schlep out to Queens at 6:30 in the morning to take the bus tour myself. And while two dozen of us wait for the bus to arrive, I meet Ali Latif. He's 60 years old, and he's been living in the Bronx with his wife for the last 14 years. His black hair is perfectly parted on one side. And he's wearing a clean Oxford shirt buttoned to the collar.
<v Wendy Dorr>12月的某个周六，我清晨6点半拖着身子去皇后区参加巴士之旅。当咱们二十几个人等着巴士到达时，我遇到了阿里·拉蒂夫。他60岁了，过去14年一直和妻子住在布朗克斯。他乌黑的头发分得很整齐。他穿着一件领口扣好的干净牛津衬衫。

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:21.540
<v Wendy Dorr>He tells me that he works as a doorman in Manhattan. And since he had to miss a day of work to come on the trip, and because he missed the bus by five minutes the week before, the trip is actually costing him $260. To be sure he doesn't miss the bus again, he's been waiting here since 5:30 this morning.
<v Wendy Dorr>他告诉我他在曼哈顿做门卫。由于他不得不请一天假来参加这次旅行，而且前一周他晚到了5分钟错过了巴士，这次旅行实际上让他损失了260美元。为了确保不再错过巴士，他从今天凌晨5点半就一直在这儿等着。

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:36.240
<v Wendy Dorr>And when he starts talking about why he's thinking of moving to Schenectady, he repeats over and over, his voice raised, pointing his long, thin finger at my nose, that he's not on a joy ride. He's a serious businessman, looking for an opportunity.
<v Wendy Dorr>当他开始谈论为什么考虑搬到斯克内克塔迪时，他一遍又一遍地重复，声音提高了，用他细长的手指指着我的鼻子，说他不是来兜风的。他是个严肃的商人，在寻找机会。

00:44:36.240 --> 00:44:46.700
<v Ali Latif>So I lose two day's pay just to go check this place out. It means that I'm not joking. $260 are losing, right?
<v Ali Latif>所以我损失了两天的工资只是为了去看看那个地方。这意味着我不是在开玩笑。损失了260美元，对吧？

00:44:46.700 --> 00:45:02.280
<v Ali Latif>I am going here on business. I am personally going here on business. I am a serious chap. That is what I'm saying. I don't make joke with business.
<v Ali Latif>我是去做生意的。我亲自去那里做生意。我是一个严肃的人。这就是我所说的。我不拿生意开玩笑。

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:29.700
<v Wendy Dorr>It's a three-hour bus ride to Schenectady. And when we pull into town, it's not pretty. We pass abandoned gas stations and empty store fronts and ugly, rundown apartment buildings. And then, in the middle of nothing, is City Hall. And City Hall is spectacular, obviously just renovated with gleaming marble, the kind of old government building that's so beautiful, that the thought crosses your mind, "Maybe I should work in government."
<v Wendy Dorr>去斯克内克塔迪有三个小时的车程。当我们进入城镇时，景象并不美好。我们经过废弃的加油站、空荡荡的店面和丑陋、破旧的公寓楼。然后，在荒凉的中间，是市政厅。市政厅非常壮观，显然刚装修过，闪闪发光的大理石，那种古老的政府大楼，太漂亮了，以至于你脑海中闪过一个念头，“也许我应该在政府工作。”

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:37.010
<v Wendy Dorr>And there, lined up in front as our bus pulls up, are a chorus of clean, perky white men in multi-colored golf shirts.
<v Wendy Dorr>当我们的大巴停下时，在前面排成一排的是一队穿着色彩鲜艳高尔夫衫、整洁、活泼的白人。

00:45:37.010 --> 00:45:38.740
<v Man>It's a long way, isn't it?
<v Man>路很远，对吧？

00:45:38.740 --> 00:45:49.960
<v Man>We've got beautiful weather for you here today, if nothing else, huh? Good morning. You have a nice ride up? These are the important guys with the food, right? Come on up.
<v Man>今天这里天气不错，如果说还有什么值得庆幸的话，对吧？早上好。旅途愉快吗？这些是带着食物的重要人物，对吧？上来吧。

00:45:49.960 --> 00:46:04.730
<v Wendy Dorr>We are led like little, sleepy sheep into an intimidating-looking room with leather chairs lining the perimeter. Everybody looks a little scared. Ali, the serious businessman who is not on a joy ride, picks a seat close to the front.
<v Wendy Dorr>我们像一群昏昏欲睡的小绵羊被带进了一个令人望而生畏的房间，房间四周排满了皮椅。每个人看起来都有点害怕。阿里，那个不是来兜风的严肃商人，选了个靠前的位置坐下。

00:46:04.730 --> 00:46:19.490
<v Man>Good morning. I want to welcome you. It's my job to talk a little bit about the general economy, the history of Schenectady. Schenectady was founded about 300 years ago as a Dutch trading settlement.
<v Man>早上好。我想欢迎大家。我的工作是谈谈斯克内克塔迪的一般经济和历史。斯克内克塔迪大约是在300年前作为荷兰贸易定居点建立的。

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:36.010
<v Wendy Dorr>This speech, by the city's Director of Economic Development, pretty much sets the tone for what's to follow all day, mind-numbingly boring facts, which go on and on and on, peppered with the kinds of utterly sincere-sounding appeals that you almost never hear from any government official, anywhere.
<v Wendy Dorr>市经济发展局局长的这次演讲，基本上奠定了接下来一整天的基调：令人头昏脑胀的无聊事实，接连不断，其中穿插着那种你几乎永远不会从任何政府官员那里听到的、听起来极其真诚的呼吁。

00:46:36.010 --> 00:46:50.040
<v Man>--because quite frankly, you're a great opportunity for us. And please know that we want you and need you. And so we hope you find us as much of an opportunity as we find you.
<v Man>——因为坦率地说，你们对我们来说是一个巨大的机会。请相信我们想要你们，也需要你们。所以我们希望你们像我们看待你们一样，看待我们也同样是一个机会。

00:46:50.040 --> 00:47:00.250
<v Wendy Dorr>One by one, the men take the stage, the head of the Chamber of Commerce, a state legislator, the president of one of the local hospitals, and the guy who owns the Goodyear Family Tire Center.
<v Wendy Dorr>男人们一个个走上台，商会会长、州议员、当地一家医院的院长，还有古德伊尔家庭轮胎中心的老板。

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:04.640
<v Man>So it truly is a very, very exciting time to be a small person in downtown Schenectady.
<v Man>所以，作为一名小人物生活在斯克内克塔迪市中心，确实是一个非常非常令人兴奋的时刻。

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:19.820
<v Wendy Dorr>The Guyanese seem to find all of this captivating. A few of them are taking notes, and everyone is alert and attentive. Ali explains to me later, they're immigrants. No official of the United States government has ever courted them like this.
<v Wendy Dorr>圭亚那人似乎觉得这一切都很引人入胜。其中几个人在做笔记，每个人都很警觉和专注。阿里后来向我解释，他们是移民。美国政府没有任何官员曾经这样追求过他们。

00:47:19.820 --> 00:47:43.610
<v Ali Latif>They didn't welcome me in that way when I reached America in 1988. They didn't tell me anything about, I welcome you because you're a hard-working man, Guyanese people. But Schenectady actually, Schenectady is crying for help. And the only people who can put Schenectady back on its foot is the Guyanese people.
<v Ali Latif>1988年我到达美国时，他们并没有那样欢迎我。他们没告诉我任何关于我欢迎你们是因为你们是努力工作的圭亚那人的话。但斯克内克塔迪实际上，斯克内克塔迪正在哭喊着寻求帮助。唯一能让斯克内克塔迪站起来的人就是圭亚那人。

00:47:43.610 --> 00:48:00.520
<v Ali Latif>It's true. It's not false. They're hard-working, and they're going to make it if they're going to put back the city on its foot.
<v Ali Latif>这是真的。不是假的。他们很勤奋，如果他们能让这座城市重新站起来，他们会成功的。

00:48:00.520 --> 00:48:17.090
<v Wendy Dorr>Which brings us to Mayor Al, who orchestrated this whole morning to get them to see it this way. He's the last speaker. And when he comes on, more than anyone else, he treats the Guyanese like they're special. He solicits them in a way that seems more genuine.
<v Wendy Dorr>这就引出了阿尔市长，他策划了这整个早晨，让他们以这种方式看待它。他是最后一位发言者。当他上台时，他比任何人都更把圭亚那人当作特别的客人对待。他以一种看起来更真诚的方式招揽他们。

00:48:17.090 --> 00:48:28.810
<v Al Jurczynski>Just in a couple minutes, I just want to tell you, Schenectady is a beautiful city. and I'm going to pass some cards out, too. If you want to just take one and pass it around.
<v Al Jurczynski>只需要几分钟，我只想告诉你们，斯克内克塔迪是一座美丽的城市。我还要发一些名片。如果你们想拿一张就传着看看。

00:48:28.810 --> 00:48:47.540
<v Wendy Dorr>He hands around a stack of his own business cards, which people seem a little shocked to be getting. And then, to make them feel welcome, he says in various ways, I am like you. He talks about his own immigrant roots, about how his grandparents came over from Poland, and about how his wife's parents moved to Schenectady from Italy.
<v Wendy Dorr>他递出一叠自己的名片，人们似乎有点惊讶会收到这些。然后，为了让他们感到受欢迎，他以各种方式说，我像你们一样。他谈到自己移民的根源，谈到他的祖父母是如何从波兰过来的，以及他妻子的父母是如何从意大利搬到斯克内克塔迪的。

00:48:47.540 --> 00:49:05.610
<v Al Jurczynski>And there's a lot of similarities between Italians and Guyanese. They're very family-oriented. They're very ambitious. It's not uncommon for the Italians to work the way the Guyanese work.
<v Al Jurczynski>意大利人和圭亚那人有很多相似之处。他们都非常以家庭为中心。他们都很有野心。意大利人像圭亚那人那样工作是很常见的。

00:49:05.610 --> 00:49:21.620
<v Al Jurczynski>I think you're a lot like Polish people, my family, in that you're very reserved. Italians like to argue. I know because my wife is Italian.
<v Al Jurczynski>我认为你们很像波兰人，像我的家人，因为你们非常内敛。意大利人喜欢争论。我知道这一点，因为我妻子是意大利人。

00:49:21.620 --> 00:49:37.000
<v Wendy Dorr>The mayor's bus tour of town takes four hours. He shows us good neighborhoods and bad, the sewage treatment plant and the old GE plant. We even stop at his son's Little League picnic in the park for half an hour, because he promised his wife he'd drop by.
<v Wendy Dorr>市长的城镇巴士之旅花了四个小时。他带我们看好坏街区，污水处理厂和老通用电气厂。我们甚至在公园里停留了半小时，参加他儿子的小联盟野餐，因为他答应妻子会顺道去看看。

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:42.060
<v Al Jurczynski>Hey, Tom. Hey, Tom.
<v Al Jurczynski>嘿，汤姆。嘿，汤姆。

00:49:42.060 --> 00:50:12.850
<v Wendy Dorr>And he's constantly dragging the citizens of Schenectady onto the bus for testimonials. Tom is a city councilman who happened to be working in his front yard. We also meet Eddie, another city employee, and Jewel, the secretary to the former mayor. And around two hours into the tour, we're driving down a long, narrow street, when Mayor Al spots a Guyanese family outside a house. I can see the mayor just about restrain himself from pointing excitedly at them.
<v Wendy Dorr>他不断把斯克内克塔迪的市民拉上巴士来做证明。汤姆是一位恰好在前院工作的市议员。我们还见了艾迪，另一位市政府员工，还有朱厄尔，前任市长的秘书。在旅行大约两个小时后，我们沿着一条狭窄的长街行驶，阿尔市长在房子外面发现了一户圭亚那家庭。我能看到市长几乎在克制自己，不要兴奋地指着他们。

00:50:12.850 --> 00:50:16.110
<v Al Jurczynski>Well, just tell everybody what you think of Schenectady.
<v Al Jurczynski>好吧，告诉大家你觉得斯克内克塔迪怎么样。

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:18.180
<v Man>Very nice place to live.
<v Man>是一个很好的居住地方。

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:41.060
<v Wendy Dorr>The last stop on the tour is the one that closes the deal for a lot of people. The mayor takes us to his wife's parents' house. Next to a small white ranch home is a huge vegetable garden, with the most beautiful vegetables I think I have ever seen in my life. It strikes a chord with everyone. The mayor's in-laws, an elderly Sicilian couple, make a point of greeting each people and shaking their hand.
<v Wendy Dorr>旅行的最后一站是让许多人成交的那一站。市长带我们去了他岳父母家。在一座小型白色牧场风格住宅旁边，是一个巨大的蔬菜园，有我这辈子见过的最漂亮的蔬菜。这触动了每个人的心弦。市长的姻亲，一对年迈的西西里夫妇，特意问候每个人并与他们握手。

00:50:41.060 --> 00:50:46.170
<v Wendy Dorr>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Wendy Dorr>[嘈杂声]

00:50:46.170 --> 00:50:56.670
<v Wendy Dorr>They offer us all some homemade wine, and we're all goners, including Rohini and Gandhi Gopi, two New York City public school teachers who were skeptical after the morning presentation.
<v Wendy Dorr>他们请我们所有人喝自制的葡萄酒，我们都被俘虏了，包括罗希尼和甘地·戈皮，两名纽约市公立学校教师，他们在早上的介绍会后还持怀疑态度。

00:50:56.670 --> 00:51:04.450
<v Rohini Gopi>When I see this, it reminds me of my own vegetable garden. And to my mind, this is the best part of the trip.
<v Rohini Gopi>当我看到这个时，让我想起了我自己的菜园。在我看来，这是这次旅行中最棒的部分。

00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:06.240
<v Wendy Dorr>So now, how are you feeling about Schenectady?
<v Wendy Dorr>所以现在，你对斯克内克塔迪感觉如何？

00:51:06.240 --> 00:51:07.310
<v Rohini Gopi>More and more positive.
<v Rohini Gopi>越来越积极了。

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:20.490
<v Gandhi Gopi>We want to get, we want to start making steps, if we could talk to somebody today about certain steps of making a move.
<v Gandhi Gopi>我们想，如果我们今天能和某人谈谈关于搬家的具体步骤，我们想开始采取行动。

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:39.680
<v Wendy Dorr>We stand around in the driveway, drinking sweet white wine out of plastic cups. When I check in on Ali, he's unbuttoned the top button of his shirt and feeling a little tipsy, and making plans to buy a house plus an empty lot next door.
<v Wendy Dorr>我们站在车道上，用塑料杯喝着甜白葡萄酒。当我查看阿里时，他解开了衬衫最上面的扣子，感觉有点微醺，正在计划买一栋房子加上隔壁的一块空地。

00:51:39.680 --> 00:51:58.840
<v Wendy Dorr>It's a month after the bus trip, and I'm back up in Schenectady. There's an old bar called Sark's in Hamilton Hill, which is the neighborhood that the mayor wants all the Guyanese to move to. You could say it's the worst neighborhood in the city, or you could say it's where the best house bargains are, depending on your point of view.
<v Wendy Dorr>巴士旅行一个月后，我又回到了斯克内克塔迪。汉密尔顿山有一家老酒吧叫萨克斯，那是市长想让所有圭亚那人搬去的社区。你可以说那是城里最糟糕的社区，或者你可以说那是房产最划算的地方，取决于你的观点。

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:21.710
<v Wendy Dorr>When you drive through it, it seems like a third of the houses are boarded up, a sketchy enough place that people in the bar are kind of surprised that I would be walking in there alone. I talk to a guy named Rod Smith. He's a carpenter, and he grew up here. And though he and his friends don't think much of the mayor, they like this one initiative. Nearly everyone I meet in Schenectady likes it.
<v Wendy Dorr>当你开车经过时，似乎三分之一的房子都用木板封起来了，这是一个相当危险的地方，以至于酒吧里的人对我独自走进去感到有点惊讶。我和一个叫罗德·史密斯的家伙谈过。他是个木匠，在这儿长大。虽然他和朋友们对市长没什么好感，但他们喜欢这个计划。我在斯克内克塔迪遇到的几乎每个人都喜欢它。

00:52:21.710 --> 00:52:49.980
<v Rod Smith>Anything to upgrade this community would be 100%. Schenectady is in the dumper right now. I can't make from my car to my front door, which is 15 feet away, without somebody trying to sell me something. I have people running up and down in front of my windows all night long, selling drugs and other things.
<v Rod Smith>升级这个社区的一切都是100%好的。斯克内克塔迪现在处于谷底。我从车到我的前门，那15英尺的路程，不能不遇到有人试图向我兜售东西。整晚都有人在我的窗户前跑来跑去，卖毒品和其他东西。

00:52:49.980 --> 00:53:12.350
<v Rod Smith>I got a guy standing outside of my front door, trying to sell drugs. And he thinks that I'm pulling over to buy something from him. And I said "I live here. Get away from my front door." Next thing I know, they're going to be coming through it. I don't want that.
<v Rod Smith>有个家伙站在我的前门外试图卖毒品。他以为我靠边停车是要从他那儿买东西。我说“我就住这儿。离我的前门远点。”接下来我怕他们要闯进来了。我可不想要那样。

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:37.160
<v Wendy Dorr>All the Guyanese believe it's safer in Schenectady than in New York City. But they're moving to the worst neighborhood in town, and the crime stats aren't encouraging. In the year 2000, Schenectady county had half the violent crimes per capita than Queens county had. But there were so many property crimes that overall, Schenectady had much higher crime rates per capita than Queens.
<v Wendy Dorr>所有的圭亚那人都相信在斯克内克塔迪比在纽约市安全。但他们正搬到城里最糟糕的社区，犯罪统计数据并不令人鼓舞。2000年，斯克内克塔迪县的人均暴力犯罪率只有皇后区的一半。但那里的财产犯罪太多，总的来说，斯克内克塔迪的人均犯罪率远高于皇后区。

00:53:37.160 --> 00:54:05.730
<v Wendy Dorr>Not that this makes much difference to the Guyanese I talk to. Whenever I ask them about the difficulties of moving, how dangerous the neighborhood is, the availability of jobs, the fact that Schenectady gets 60 inches of snow a year, so they're not going to actually get that much time in their gardens, they shrug it off. They say what I imagine immigrants to this city have always said. They say they're ready to sacrifice, which is one reason the mayor's pitch seems to work so well.
<v Wendy Dorr>并不是说这对我和我交谈的圭亚那人有什么影响。每当我问他们搬迁的困难、社区有多危险、就业机会、斯克内克塔迪一年降雪60英寸（所以他们实际上不会在花园里花那么多时间）等事实时，他们都耸耸肩。他们说了我想象中搬到这个城市的移民一直说的话。他们说他们准备好做出牺牲，这也是市长的推销策略似乎如此有效的原因之一。

00:54:05.730 --> 00:54:20.550
<v Wendy Dorr>He doesn't tell them it's going to be easy in Schenectady. He makes it clear it's going to be hard, and they're going to have to work hard. And they all tell me, that's exactly what they're prepared to do.
<v Wendy Dorr>他没告诉他们斯克内克塔迪会很轻松。他明确表示这会很艰难，他们必须努力工作。他们都告诉我，这正是他们准备好要做的。

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:30.310
<v Ali Latif>This house, look at it. The driveway is very wide. Right? It's very spacious, wide. It's not choked up--
<v Ali Latif>这房子，看看它。车道非常宽。对吧？非常宽敞，开阔。没有堵塞——

00:54:30.310 --> 00:54:57.220
<v Wendy Dorr>When I catch up with Ali, he shows me a Polaroid of the yellow house he now owns in Schenectady. Two weeks after our bus trip, he took another day off from work, bought a $70 bus ticket, and went up to see some houses. He liked this one immediately. He didn't have the place inspected or do any more research about Schenectady or the neighborhood. And compared to prices in New York City, it was a steal. He paid $55,000, all cash.
<v Wendy Dorr>当我见到阿里时，他给我看了他现在在斯克内克塔迪拥有的那栋黄色房子的宝丽来照片。在我们巴士旅行两周后，他又请了一天假，买了张70美元的车票，上去看了几栋房子。他立刻就看中了这一栋。他没有让人去检查那个地方，也没有做更多关于斯克内克塔迪或那个社区的调查。相比纽约市的价格，这简直是抢到了。他花了55000美元，全是现金。

00:54:57.220 --> 00:54:59.670
<v Ali Latif>The house is profit, because people--
<v Ali Latif>房子是有利的，因为人们——

00:54:59.670 --> 00:55:21.280
<v Wendy Dorr>It's a two-family house, and there are two tenants in the building. The tenants are section eight, which means that the government pays a large portion of the rent. He'll rent it out for two years, and then he'll move to Schenectady to retire. He's put his faith and his life savings into this plan. And it was all because of the mayor, he tells me, the way the mayor treated him.
<v Wendy Dorr>这是一栋双户住宅，楼里有两个租户。租户是第八节援助对象，这意味着政府支付大部分租金。他会把房子出租两年，然后他会搬到斯克内克塔迪退休。他把他的信念和他一生的积蓄都投在了这个计划上。这一切都是因为市长，他告诉我，是因为市长对待他的方式。

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:35.160
<v Ali Latif>I think one, why I'm going to live here, I can approach him. That's a most important factor. I can't go approach Mr. Bush and Rudy Giuliani and the governor.
<v Ali Latif>我想第一，我为什么要去那里住，因为我可以接近他。这是一个最重要的因素。我不能接近布什先生和鲁迪·朱利安尼还有州长。

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:48.790
<v Ali Latif>I can't well meet them. It's hard to reach them. But I can reach, if I live in Schenectady, I could go and reach the mayor. And I am sure they would listen to me.
<v Ali Latif>我很难见到他们。很难接触到他们。但如果我住在斯克内克塔迪，我就能接近，我就能去找市长。而且我敢肯定他们会听我的。

00:55:48.790 --> 00:55:52.680
<v Al Jurczynski>These are people that are from Guyana originally--
<v Al Jurczynski>这些人最初是来自圭亚那的——

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:54.630
<v Wendy Dorr>Again, Mayor Al.
<v Wendy Dorr>又是阿尔市长。

00:55:54.630 --> 00:56:33.270
<v Al Jurczynski>--but for the last 10 or 15 years, have been in New York City. And in New York City, they revere the office of mayor. The mayor of New York City is an international figure. So when they come here, oftentimes they'll say, "I've never shaken the hand of a mayor before." And I go along with that. I let them think that I'm bigger than life.
<v Al Jurczynski>——但在过去的10到15年里，他们一直在纽约市。而在纽约市，他们崇拜市长这个职位。纽约市市长是一个国际人物。所以当他们来到这里时，他们经常会说，“我以前从没和市长握过手。”我就顺着他们说。我让他们觉得我是一个了不起的人物。

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:57.240
<v Wendy Dorr>He says this is completely different from the way his regular constituents greet him. It's a hard, blue-collar city, he says. The old residents don't put the mayor up on a pedestal. Only the new residents do that, which is a mixed blessing for him, since he's not the sort of person who feels very comfortable on a pedestal.
<v Wendy Dorr>他说这与他的普通选民欢迎他的方式完全不同。他说，这是一座艰难的蓝领城市。老居民不会把市长放在神坛上。只有新居民才会那样做，这对他是种混合的祝福，因为他不是那种在神坛上感觉很自在的人。

00:56:57.240 --> 00:57:30.930
<v Ira Glass>Wendy Dorr. As best as anybody knows, there were about 1,000 Guyanese in Schenectady before the mayor started his initiative in May. And according to mortgage brokers, perhaps 200 families, maybe 1,000 people have moved since then. The mayor's goal is to triple the Guyanese community to 3,000 people by the end of the year. His long-term goal is a little more ambitious, 35,000 people by the end of three years, a huge increase for a city of 60,000. So far it's a small enough change that everybody that Wendy talked to in Schenectady, even in the neighborhood that is most affected, told her they did not notice a change yet.
<v Ira Glass>温迪·多尔。据所知，在市长5月份启动计划之前，斯克内克塔迪大约有1000名圭亚那人。据抵押贷款经纪人说，从那时起可能有200个家庭，也许1000人已经搬过来了。市长的目标是在今年年底将圭亚那社区增加到3000人。他的长期目标更有野心一点，三年内达到35000人，这对一个6万人的城市来说是巨大的增长。到目前为止，变化还很小，温迪在斯克内克塔迪采访的每个人，即使是在受影响最严重的社区，也告诉她他们还没有感觉到变化。

00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:51.820
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "MOSES SUPPOSES" BY GENE KELLY AND DONALD O'CONNOR]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - 基因·凯利和唐纳德·奥康纳的《摩西假设》]

00:57:51.820 --> 00:58:15.200
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Wendy Dorr and myself with Alex Blumberg, Jonathan Goldstein, Starlee Kine, and Mr. Chris Neary. Senior producer Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Jane Golembiski. Music help today from Agoraphone. Our website, where you can listen to our programs for free or order tapes, www.thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由温迪·多尔和我自己，以及亚历克斯·布隆伯格、乔纳森·戈德斯坦、斯塔莉·凯恩和克里斯·尼里先生共同制作。高级制作人朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。 Todd Bachmann和Jane Golembiski提供制作协助。 Agoraphone提供音乐协助。我们的网站，您可以在那里免费收听我们的节目或订购磁带，www.thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播国际发行。

00:58:15.200 --> 00:58:18.190
